+  <section id="background">
+    <p class="section-kicker">01 · Background</p>
+    <h2>The problem begins with who owns the work</h2>
+    <span class="skip-note">Know the mirror lifecycle already? Skip to “The narrow problem.”</span>
+
+    <h3>Deep background: a mirror is created in two phases</h3>
+    <p>Entire stores a repository in regional infrastructure. When the CLI asks for a mirror, the system first establishes a <strong>placement</strong>: a durable record saying which regional cluster owns the repository. The cluster then performs the slower work needed to make the Git repository usable. Those are different facts. A placement can exist while the clone is still being prepared.</p>
+    <p>The CLI therefore has to answer two questions in order: “Where will this mirror live?” and “Is that mirror ready yet?” The second question was already asynchronous before this pull request. After the server returned a placement, the CLI polled the mirror every two seconds until its state became ready or terminal.</p>
+
+    <div class="figure">
+      <p class="figure-title">The lifecycle the user cares about</p>
+      <p class="figure-note">Example: place entireio/cli in the us-east-2 cluster, then wait until it is cloneable.</p>
+      <div class="flow">
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">CLI intent</span><code>entire repo mirror create<br>aws-us-east-2</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Placement</span><code>mirror_id = m_42<br>cluster = us-east-2</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Preparation</span><code>state = cloning</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Usable result</span><code>state = ready<br>ssh://…/cli.git</code></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <p>This distinction matters because the command exposes <code>--no-wait</code>. “Do not wait” does not mean “fire an unvalidated packet and exit.” It means “wait until placement is known, print its URL, and leave clone readiness to the caller.” Without a placement there is no useful result to return.</p>
+
+    <h3>The narrow problem: the first phase was still synchronous</h3>
+    <p>Before this change, the CLI called the legacy mirror-create endpoint and kept that HTTP request open while the control plane arranged placement. If the connection disappeared, cancellation could propagate into work that the user expected the server to own. The client also had no durable request identifier it could poll after losing the original response.</p>
+    <p>The server-side contract added by <a href="https://github.com/entirehq/entiredb/pull/3441">entiredb PR #3441 (coreapi: add asynchronous mirror request routes)</a> changes that boundary. Submission returns HTTP 202 and a <code>Location</code> header for a persistent request. The server can continue processing independently; the CLI follows the receipt until it contains a successful placement or a structured failure.</p>
+
+    <div class="split figure">
+      <div class="lane">
+        <span class="lane-label">Before: connection owns phase one</span>
+        <ol>
+          <li>CLI opens <code>POST /mirrors</code>.</li>
+          <li>Request stays open during placement.</li>
+          <li>Response finally returns a mirror.</li>
+          <li>CLI polls clone readiness.</li>
+        </ol>
+      </div>
+      <div class="lane">
+        <span class="lane-label">After: request record owns phase one</span>
+        <ol>
+          <li>CLI opens <code>POST /mirror-requests</code>.</li>
+          <li>Server returns <code>202 + Location</code>.</li>
+          <li>CLI polls the durable request.</li>
+          <li>CLI reuses the same clone-readiness poll.</li>
+        </ol>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="callout good">
+      <span class="callout-label">The governing invariant</span>
+      <p>After a successful submission, placement work belongs to the server-side request—not to the lifetime of the submitting HTTP connection.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <h3>Why this is a rollout bridge, not an endpoint replacement</h3>
+    <p><a href="https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-1185">COR-1185 (CLI: submit mirror creates through the async mirror-request routes)</a> deliberately keeps both routes. A normal layered repository setting, <code>async_mirror_requests</code>, selects the new path. Its default is false, so existing repositories retain the synchronous behavior until a project or local override enables the new contract.</p>
+    <p>Once enabled, the choice is strict: the CLI does not probe for capability and does not fall back on a 404. A missing route is reported as a failure. In effect, that makes rollout errors visible instead of quietly converting an intended durability guarantee back into the legacy behavior.</p>
+
+    <pre class="code"><span class="com">// .entire/settings.json (shared project setting)</span>
+{
+  <span class="key">"async_mirror_requests"</span>: <span class="str">true</span>
+}
+
+<span class="com">// .entire/settings.local.json may override it for one clone.</span></pre>
+
+    <p>This setting uses the repository’s ordinary project/local layering rather than a special local-only loader. There is one careful exception: <code>entire repo mirror create</code> is a control-plane command allowed to run even when the local <code>.entire</code> directory is broken. If settings cannot be loaded there, the command preserves that exemption and uses the default synchronous path rather than failing on local state.</p>
+  </section>
