+  <section id="intuition">
+    <p class="section-kicker">02 · Intuition</p>
+    <h2>Think of submission as getting a claim ticket</h2>
+
+    <p>The essence is familiar from coat checks and job queues. Handing over the item is short. The receipt is durable. Collection can be retried. The new endpoint does not make placement itself instantaneous; it gives the client a stable name for work that continues after the first connection ends.</p>
+
+    <div class="figure">
+      <p class="figure-title">A concrete async request</p>
+      <p class="figure-note">The values are illustrative, but the state transitions match the generated API contract.</p>
+      <div class="timeline">
+        <div class="state"><b>1. queued</b><small><code>POST</code> returns 202 and <code>Location: /api/v1/mirror-requests/7b…</code></small></div>
+        <div class="state"><b>2. placing</b><small><code>GET Location</code> returns <code>processing</code>; the control plane still owns the job.</small></div>
+        <div class="state"><b>3. cloning</b><small>The request succeeds with mirror ID and URLs; the CLI enters its existing mirror poll.</small></div>
+        <div class="state terminal"><b>4. ready</b><small>The mirror reports ready; the command prints the contract-backed URL.</small></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <p>The API request has four states—pending, processing, succeeded, and failed—but the CLI translates those into user-facing phases: queued, placing, and cloning. That translation keeps server vocabulary from leaking into every caller while still making progress intelligible.</p>
+
+    <h3>One timeout budget crosses both polls</h3>
+    <p>A subtle design choice follows from the claim-ticket model: <code>--timeout</code> is not reset after each phase. On the async path, a single context begins before submission and covers submission, placement polling, and clone polling. If the user asks for a 60-second wait, the entire operation gets 60 seconds—not 60 seconds for each step.</p>
+
+    <div class="figure">
+      <p class="figure-title">One wall-clock budget</p>
+      <p class="figure-note">Example only: the actual phase durations depend on the service.</p>
+      <div class="flow">
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">0–1 s</span><code>submit</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">1–12 s</span><code>poll placement</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">12–47 s</span><code>poll clone</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">47 s</span><code>ready within<br>60 s total</code></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <p>The command preserves the existing two-second clone poll cadence and its tolerance for fifteen consecutive poll errors—about thirty seconds. The placement poll uses the same bounded error pattern. This is especially important immediately after placement: authorization data can lag briefly, so an early 404 need not turn a successful placement into a failed command.</p>
+
+    <h3>The Location header is both a receipt and a trust boundary</h3>
+    <p>The CLI does not blindly fetch whatever URL appears in <code>Location</code>. It accepts only HTTP(S) URLs whose path is exactly a mirror-request resource with a UUID, and rejects user information, query strings, fragments, malformed paths, and scheme-relative hosts. That prevents a server response from turning the authenticated client into a general-purpose URL fetcher.</p>
+    <p>Cross-jurisdiction routing adds another wrinkle. A request may first contact one core, receive a 421 redirect signal, exchange credentials, and retry against the repository’s home core. The server can return a relative <code>Location</code>. The transport therefore anchors that location to the <em>effective</em> request URL after routing, so the poll goes to the same home core with the right token.</p>
+
+    <div class="figure">
+      <p class="figure-title">Why Location canonicalization belongs in the transport</p>
+      <p class="figure-note">The caller sees one trustworthy absolute poll target after cross-jurisdiction retry.</p>
+      <div class="flow">
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Initial target</span><code>core A<br>control token</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">421 routing</span><code>home = core B<br>exchange token</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">202 response</span><code>Location:<br>/api/v1/mirror-requests/7b…</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Canonical target</span><code>https://core-b/…/7b…</code></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <h3>Truthful output beats a plausible guess</h3>
+    <p>The legacy response tells the CLI whether the mirror was newly registered or already existed. The async success contract returns the mirror’s identity and URLs, but no equivalent “created” bit. The CLI therefore prints neutral language—<code>Mirror placed at …</code>—rather than guessing from circumstances. The URL is contract-backed; the creation history is not.</p>
+
+    <div class="callout warn">
+      <span class="callout-label">Important edge case</span>
+      <p><code>--no-wait</code> on the async path still waits through placement. It skips only clone readiness, because the placement result is what provides the mirror URL.</p>
+    </div>
+  </section>
